{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve \\((3^{-1} + 5^{-1})^{-1} \\pmod{31}\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the modular inverses of 3 and 5 modulo 31.\n2. Add these inverses together modulo 31.\n3. Find the modular inverse of the result from step 2 modulo 31.\n\n### Step 1: Find the modular inverses\n\nThe modular inverse of \\(a\\) modulo \\(m\\) is a number \\(b\\) such that \\(a \\cdot b \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m}\\).\n\n#### Inverse of 3 modulo 31\nWe need to find \\(x\\) such that \\(3x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{31}\\). We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm for this.\n\nApplying the Euclidean algorithm:\n\\[\n31 = 10 \\cdot 3 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 31 - 10 \\cdot 3\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n3 \\cdot (-10) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{31}\n\\]\nSo, the inverse of 3 modulo 31 is \\(-10\\). Since we want a positive integer, we convert \\(-10\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 31:\n\\[\n-10 + 31 = 21\n\\]\nThus, \\(3^{-1} \\equiv 21 \\pmod{31}\\).\n\n#### Inverse of 5 modulo 31\nWe need to find \\(y\\) such that \\(5y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{31}\\). Again, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\n","prefix_end":984,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,1124,1188,18,87210,16,92,488,220,20,87210,16,5410,87210,16,92,1124,79,2593,90,18,16,11035,701,582,1184,311,1795,1493,7354,1447,16,13,7379,279,43893,64583,288,315,220,18,323,220,20,59864,220,18,16,624,17,13,2691,1493,64583,288,3786,59864,220,18,16,624,18,13,7379,279,43893,27949,315,279,1102,504,3019,220,17,59864,220,18,16,382,14374,14822,220,16,25,7379,279,43893,64583,288,271,785,43893,27949,315,17767,64,57758,59864,17767,76,57758,374,264,1372,17767,65,57758,1741,429,17767,64,1124,50853,293,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,76,11035,3593,820,758,4450,315,220,18,59864,220,18,16,198,1654,1184,311,1477,17767,87,57758,1741,429,17767,18,87,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,18,16,11035,568,1205,646,990,279,40565